016  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForms | Superior Court of California | County of Orange (https://www.occourts.org/forms-filing/forms)\nciteturn1168view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1132view1\",\"lineno\":328}); Total lines: 734\nL302:       * cite293†Court Telephone Numbers L303:     * cite294†Media Relations L304:       * cite295†Cameras in the Court L305:       * cite18†Courtroom Live Streaming L306:       * cite296†Rule 10.500 Access to Judicial Administrative Records L307:     * cite297†News & Events L308:       * cite298†Appeals & Records COVID-19 Response L309:       * cite299†Archive of Notices L310:       * cite300†Criminal & Traffic COVID-19 Response L311:       * cite301†Past News Releases L312:       * cite302†Civil Video Appearances L313:       * cite303†Family Law & Family Court Services COVID-19 Response L314:       * cite304†Jury Service COVID-19 Response L315:       * cite305†Juvenile Court COVID-19 Response L316:       * cite306†Probate & Mental Health COVID-19 Response L317:       * cite307†Self-Help Services COVID-19 Response L318:     * cite308†Program Resource List L319:     * cite309†Records L320:     * cite310†Remote Appearance Information L321:     * cite311†Reports Pursuant to Government Code 9795 L322:     * cite312†Temporary Judge Program L323:     * cite313†Terms and Conditions L324:     * cite314†General Information L325:   * cite315†Court Leadership L326: \nL327: Search [Input: Search] Search\nL328: # Forms\nL329: \nL330: In this section\nL331: \nL332:   * cite40†Forms by Packet L333:   * cite41†Unlawful Detainer Local Forms L334: ## Access to Forms\nL335: \nL336: The Superior Court of Orange County offers several options to assist the public in completing court forms:\nL337:   * cite316†State Judicial Council Approved Forms†www.courts.ca.gov : All statewide approved forms are available for Adoptions, Civil, Conservatorships, Criminal, Guardianships, Family Law, Juvenile, Name Change, Probate, Small Claims, and Traffic.\nL338:   * cite317†Locally Approved Forms : In addition to forms approved by the State Judicial Council, the Superior Court of Orange County has approved a variety of forms, some of which are fillable, giving you a larger selection of forms to assist you.\nL339:   * cite318†Locally Approved Form Packets: In addition to the Court's Self-Help Centers form packets, which contain the required State Judicial Council Approved Forms and Locally Approved forms for various case types, the various case types have case-type specific form packets created to assist the public in completing these forms.\nL340:   * cite319†Self-Help Form Packets : The Court's Self-Help Centers provide a variety of form packets (which contain the required State Judicial Council Approved Forms and Locally Approved forms for various case types). Also, our Self-Help Centers can provide form packets, document review and workshops to self-represented litigants.\nL341: ### Additional Types\nL342: \nL343:   * #### cite316†State Judicial Council Approved Forms†www.courts.ca.gov L344: \nL345:   * #### cite320†Self-Help Form Packets L346: \nL347:   * #### cite321†LOCALLY APPROVED FORM PACKETS L348: \nL349: ## Locally Approved Forms\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFiling and Forms | United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit (https://www.ca9.uscourts.gov/filing)\nciteturn1168view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1132view3\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 108\nL0: cite0†United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit L1: \nL2: Chief Judge Mary H. Murguia\nL3: \nL4: [Button: Menu]\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Image: Close L7: \nL8:   * cite2†Decisions L9:   * cite3†Cases and Hearings L10:   * cite4†Filing and Forms L11:   * cite5†For Attorneys L12:   * cite6†Rules L13:   * cite7†Guides and Resources L14:   * cite8†Mediation L15:   * cite9†About L16: \nL17:   * cite10†Courts of the Ninth Circuit†www.ce9.uscourts.gov L18:   * cite11†Bankruptcy Appellate Panel†www.bap9.uscourts.gov L19: \nL20: Search [Input] cite12†Image: Search L21: # Filing and Forms\nL22: \nL23: “Filing” is the process of giving the Clerk of the Court an official document to enter into a case’s files or records. Often, you’ll use a form to do so.\nL24: \nL25: ## Filing Options\nL26: \nL27: ### If you're an attorney or court reporter\nL28: \nL29: You must file documents electronically using cite13†ACMS .\nL30: \nL31: cite14†LEARN ABOUT E-FILING L32: ### If you're representing yourself\nL33: \nL34: You can file documents:\nL35: \nL36:   * Electronically through cite13†ACMS L37: \nL38:   * Electronically through the court's cite15†online submission system L39: \nL40:   * By U.S. mail\nL41: \nL42:   * By dropping them off at the Browning Courthouse in San Francisco\nL43: \nL44: cite16†LEARN MORE L45: \nL46: ## Motions\nL47: \nL48: If you want to ask the court to do something in your case, you need to file a motion. A motion is a written request of the court.\nL49: \nL50: cite17†LEARN ABOUT MOTIONS L51: ## Briefs\nL52: \nL53: A brief is a written document that explains a party’s legal and factual arguments.\nL54: \nL55: cite18†LEARN ABOUT BRIEFS L56: \nL57: ## Excerpts of Record\nL58: \nL59: Excerpts of record are a set of lower court documents that the parties compile and file with the appeals court.\nL60: \nL61: cite19†LEARN ABOUT EXCERPTS OF RECORD L62: \nL63: ## Sealed Documents\nL64: \nL65: If you want to file a document under seal, you must file a motion to seal or a notice of sealing.\nL66: \nL67: cite20†LEARN ABOUT SEALED DOCUMENTS --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFiling | United States Bankruptcy Appellate Panel for the Ninth Circuit (https://www.bap9.uscourts.gov/filing)\nciteturn1168view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1132view4\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 114\nL0: cite0†United States Bankruptcy Appellate Panel for the Ninth Circuit L1: \nL2: [Button: Menu]\nL3: \nL4: cite1†Image: Close†cdn.ca9.uscourts.gov L5: \nL6:   * cite2†Opinions L7:   * cite3†Calendar L8:   * cite4†Filing L9:   * cite5†Rules L10:   * cite6†Appeal Process L11:   * cite7†About L12: \nL13:   * cite8†Court of Appeals†www.ca9.uscourts.gov L14:   * cite9†Courts of the Ninth Circuit†www.ce9.uscourts.gov L15: \nL16: Search [Input] cite10†Image: Search L17: # Filing\nL18: \nL19: The Case Management/Electronic Case Filing (CM/ECF) system lets attorneys file and receive court documents online. Below you’ll find information about who can register, how to get started, and where to get help.\nL20: ## Who can Register\nL21: \nL22:   * All attorneys licensed to practice before the BAP must register for CM/ECF (unless the court grants an exemption).\nL23: \nL24:   * Others, such as parties without attorneys, must file a motion asking the court for permission to register.\nL25: \nL26:   * Each attorney must register individually. Law firms cannot register as a group.\nL27: ## How to Register\nL28: \nL29:   1. Get an individual PACER account (or upgrade your existing one).\nL30: \nL31: cite11†REGISTER FOR PACER†pacer.psc.uscourts.gov L32: \nL33:   2. Use PACER to request e-filing access for the Ninth Circuit BAP.\nL34: \nL35:   3. Wait for approval. Most requests are approved quickly, but it may take up to 7 days.\nL36: \nL37:   4. Once approved, you’ll get an email from the PACER Service Center.\nL38: \nL39: Training and step-by-step instructions are available on the PACER site. For questions, call the PACER Service Center at (800) 676-6856.\nL40: ## Costs\nL41: \nL42:   * There is no fee to register or file in CM/ECF.\nL43: \nL44:   * Viewing documents through PACER costs $0.10 per page (with one free view from the Notice of Docket Activity).\nL45: ## Filing and Service\nL46: \nL47:   * Most documents can be filed through BAP CM/ECF.\nL48: \nL49: cite12†LOG IN TO BAP CM/ECF†ecf.ca9.uscourts.gov L50: \nL51:     * Some exceptions are listed in the cite13†BAP’s Administrative Order on electronic filing†cdn.ca9.uscourts.gov (PDF).\nL52: \nL53:   * Do not send paper copies unless the court orders you to.\nL54: \nL55:   * All filings must include a Certificate of Service as the last page.\nL56: \nL57:   * If the other party is not using CM/ECF, you must serve them by mail.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForms | Central District of California | United States District Court (https://www.cacd.uscourts.gov/court-procedures/forms)\nciteturn1168view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1132view2\",\"lineno\":605}); Total lines: 652\nL587:       * cite208†Filing Locations L588:       * cite209†Sealed Filings L589:       * cite210†Violation Notices (CVB) L590:       * cite211†Fine/Restitution L591:       * cite212†Post-Indictment Arraignment L592:       * cite213†Reconveyance/Deed of Trust L593:     * cite69†Emergency Filing Procedures L594:     * cite70†Filing Fees L595:     * cite71†Letters Rogatory L596:     * cite72†Writ of Execution L597:   * cite73†Forms L598:   * cite62†General Orders L599:     * cite62†Operative by Subject Matter L600:     * cite74†Operative in Numerical Order L601:     * cite75†Archived General Orders L602:     * cite76†Index of General Orders by Subject (pdf) L603:     * cite77†Index of General Orders in Numerical Order (pdf) L604:   * cite63†Local Rules L605:   * cite73†Show All Forms L606:   * District Court Forms\nL607:     * cite214†ADR Forms L608:     * cite